.SS OBJECT
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT \f[B]struct\f[R]
#define object \f[B]struct\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Used to visually distinguish objects from regular structs.
.PP
This macro has no functional impact.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
object o_object
{
    void    (*worked)(int);
    int     value;
};
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJ
.IP
.EX
#define OBJ(OBJECT_NAME, VARIABLE_NAME)
#define obj(object_name, variable_name)
.EE
.PP
Instantiates an object \f[B]on the stack\f[R].
.PP
The variable is a plain object value, so its members are reached with
\f[CR].\f[R].
.PP
Calling \f[CR]OBJ\f[R] automatically invokes the constructor.
.PP
\f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] may safely be called on the object.
Since the object lives on the stack, the call simply does nothing after
any member cleanup has completed.
.PP
The constructor must be named exactly the same as the object type:
.IP
.EX
object o_test
{
    ...
};

void o_test()
{
    ...
}
.EE
.PP
Constructors may accept parameters:
.IP
.EX
void o_test(int arg, char *arg2)
{
    ...
}
.EE
.PP
Usage:
.IP
.EX
obj (o_test, test) ();
.EE
.PP
With parameters:
.IP
.EX
obj (o_test, test) (42, \(dqtest\(dq);
.EE
.PP
Since the macro expands to an ordinary declaration, qualifiers and
attributes may be written in front of it:
.IP
.EX
const obj (o_test, test) (42);

test.setName(\(dqtest\(dq);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS NEW
.IP
.EX
#define NEW(OBJECT_NAME, VARIABLE_NAME)
#define new(object_name, variable_name)
.EE
.PP
Instantiates an object \f[B]on the heap\f[R].
.PP
The variable is a pointer to the object, so its members are reached with
\f[CR]\->\f[R].
.PP
The allocation is checked before the constructor runs: if
\f[CR]ALLOC\f[R] fails, the variable is \f[CR]NULL\f[R] and the
constructor is never called.
.PP
Unlike \f[CR]OBJ\f[R], heap instances own their storage.
Calling \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] after member cleanup releases that
allocation.
.PP
The caller owns that allocation and has to release it with
\f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] once the injected members have been ejected.
.PP
Usage:
.IP
.EX
new (o_test, test) ();
.EE
.PP
With parameters:
.IP
.EX
new (o_test, test) (42, \(dqtest\(dq);
.EE
.PP
Because the object lives on the heap, it can outlive the scope it was
created in and be returned from a function:
.IP
.EX
void    *a_function(void)
{
    new (test_object_type, result) (1);

    \f[B]return\f[R] (result);
}
.EE
.PP
Qualifiers work here too.
Note that \f[CR]const\f[R] makes the \f[I]object\f[R] read\-only, so its
fields can no longer be assigned directly and have to go through a
method:
.IP
.EX
const new (test_object_type, test) (42);

test\->set(33);
\f[I]/* test\->value = 33; \-> error: assignment of member \(aqvalue\(aq in read\-only object */\f[R]
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS DESTROY
.IP
.EX
#define DESTROY(THE_OBJECT)
#define destroy(the_object)
.EE
.PP
Releases an object that was created \f[B]on the heap\f[R] with
\f[CR]NEW\f[R].
.PP
The argument is the object pointer itself, so from outside it is the
variable \f[CR]NEW\f[R] produced, and from inside a method it is simply
\f[CR]THIS\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
new (o_test, test) ();
\&...
destroy (test);
.EE
.IP
.EX
void    free_self(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    destroy (this);
}
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] only gives back the object allocation.
It does \f[B]not\f[R] eject anything, so every injected member has to be
ejected \f[I]before\f[R] the object is destroyed, otherwise the
executable memory behind each trampoline leaks and the ejects themselves
would be reading freed memory.
.PP
After the call the pointer is dangling; nothing in the object may be
touched again, including \f[CR]this\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Recommended usage\f[R]: since there is no automatic destructor, put
\f[CR]destroy\f[R] as the last statement of the \f[CR]free()\f[R] member
you write anyway, so a heap object cleans itself up in one call:
.IP
.EX
void    test_free(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    object__eject (this\->test);
    object__eject (this\->free); \f[I]/* Always the last eject! */\f[R]

    destroy (this); \f[I]/* Always the very last statement! */\f[R]
}
.EE
.IP
.EX
new (o_test, test) ();

test\->free(); \f[I]/* Ejects everything and frees the object */\f[R]
.EE
.SS /!\(rs Stack objects
\f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] may also be called on objects created with
\f[CR]obj\f[R].
.PP
The macro detects whether the object resides on the heap.
Heap objects are released normally; stack objects are left untouched, so
calling \f[CR]destroy\f[R] on them is a no\-op.
.PP
This allows a single cleanup path to be shared between stack and heap
instances:
.IP
.EX
void free_self(void)
{
    object__connect(o_test);

    object__eject(this\->test);
    object__eject(this\->free);

    destroy(this); \f[I]/* Safe for both stack and heap objects */\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
If the pointer was declared \f[CR]const\f[R], destroy it from inside a
method, where the \f[CR]this\f[R] produced by \f[CR]OBJECT__CONNECT\f[R]
carries no qualifier, or cast it at the call site \- depending on how
strict the compiler is about qualifiers on \f[CR]free\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJECT__CONNECT
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT__CONNECT(OBJECT_TYPE_NAME)
#define object__connect(object_type_name)
.EE
.PP
Connects a function to an object instance.
.PP
This macro defines a pointer named \f[CR]THIS\f[R] (or \f[CR]this\f[R]
in the lowercase form) that refers to the parent object.
It reads the object address straight out of the register the trampoline
placed it in, so it must be the \f[B]first statement\f[R] of the
function.
.PP
\f[B]Examples\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
void test1(int a)
{
    OBJECT__CONNECT (o_object);

    THIS\->value = a;
}

void test2(int a)
{
    object__connect (o_object);

    this\->value = a;
}
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJECT__INJECT
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT__INJECT(TARGET, SOURCE)
#define object__inject(target, source)
.EE
.PP
Used to inject a function into an object instance.
.PP
Functions are \f[B]not\f[R] automatically bound; injection must be done
manually, usually inside the constructor.
.PP
\f[CR]TARGET\f[R] is the member that will hold the bound method,
\f[CR]SOURCE\f[R] is the plain function that implements it.
Both names are given explicitly, so the member and the function do not
have to share a name:
.IP
.EX
void o_object(int a)
{
    object__connect (o_object);

    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->test, test);       \f[I]/* this\->test  = test  */\f[R]
    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->abc, test);        \f[I]/* this\->abc   = test  */\f[R]
    object__inject (struct_ptr\->abc, test);  \f[I]/* any lvalue works    */\f[R]

    \f[B]this\f[R]\->value = a;
}
.EE
.PP
Under the hood the macro assembles a small trampoline that loads the
object address into a register and jumps to \f[CR]SOURCE\f[R], then
copies it into executable memory with \f[CR]ALLOC_EXE\f[R].
That trampoline is what makes \f[CR]OBJECT__CONNECT\f[R] able to recover
\f[CR]this\f[R], and it is why every injected member owns a small
executable allocation that has to be released again with
\f[CR]OBJECT__EJECT\f[R].
.RS
.PP
The older \f[CR]OBJECT__INJECT_2\f[R] / \f[CR]OBJECT__INJECT_3\f[R]
variants no longer exist.
The two\-argument form replaces all of them.
.RE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJECT__EJECT
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT__EJECT(MEMBER_NAME)
#define object__eject(member_name)
.EE
.PP
Since no automatic destructor exists, members must be ejected manually.
.PP
A custom destructor must be implemented by the user.
.PP
Ejecting releases the trampoline behind a member, so the member that
holds the destructor itself has to be ejected \f[B]last\f[R].
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
object o_test
{
    void    (*free)(void);
    void    (*test)(void);
};

void test(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);
}

void test_free(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    object__eject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->test);
    object__eject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->free); \f[I]/* Always on the end! */\f[R]
    destroy (\f[B]this\f[R]);
}

void o_test(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->test, test);
    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->free, test_free);
}
.EE
.PP
For objects created with \f[CR]new\f[R], ejecting only gives back the
executable memory of the injected methods.
The object allocation itself still belongs to the caller and has to be
released separately.
.PP
For objects created with \f[CR]new\f[R], ejecting only gives back the
executable memory of the injected methods.
The object allocation itself is released with \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R], either
by the caller or as the last statement of the member that does the
ejecting.
.SS Full Example
.IP
.EX
#define INCL_CMT_OBJECT \f[I]/* DEFINE OOP */\f[R]
#include \f[B]\(dqCMT/CMT.H\(dq\f[R]
#include \f[B]<stdio.h>\f[R]

object test_object_type
{
    void    (*add)(int);
    void    (*set)(int);
    int     value;
    void    (*free)(void);
};

void    add(int value)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    \f[B]this\f[R]\->value += value;
}

void    set(int value)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    \f[B]this\f[R]\->value = value;
}

void    free_self(void)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    object__eject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->add);
    object__eject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->set);

    object__eject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->free); \f[I]/* Always on the end! */\f[R]
    destroy (\f[B]this\f[R]);
}

void    test_object_type(int start_var)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->add, add);
    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->set, set);
    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->free, free_self);
    \f[B]this\f[R]\->value = start_var;
}

void    *a_function(void)
{
    \f[B]new\f[R] (test_object_type, result) (1); \f[I]/* Heap */\f[R]

    \f[B]return\f[R] (result);
}

int main(void)
{
    object test_object_type *a;

    const \f[B]new\f[R] (test_object_type, test_h) (42); \f[I]/* Yeah, variable attributes works too */\f[R]
    obj (test_object_type, test_s) (22); \f[I]/* Stack */\f[R]

    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_h\->value); \f[I]/* 42 */\f[R]
    test_h\->add(42);
    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_h\->value); \f[I]/* 84 */\f[R]
    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_s.value); \f[I]/* 22 */\f[R]

    test_s.value = 33;
    test_h\->set(33); \f[I]/* I needed to do this in C++ way. */\f[R]
    \f[I]/* test_h\->value = 33; | error: assignment of member \(aqvalue\(aq in read\-only object */\f[R]

    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_h\->value); \f[I]/* 33 */\f[R]
    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_s.value); \f[I]/* 33 */\f[R]

    test_h\->free();
    test_s.free();      \f[I]/* Stack: nothing else to release */\f[R]

    a = a_function();
    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, a\->value); \f[I]/* 1 */\f[R]
    a\->free();
    \f[B]return\f[R] (0);
}
.EE
.SS Notes
Trampolines are currently generated for \f[B]Intel\f[R] CPUs in 16, 32
and 64\-bit mode.
ARM and PowerPC are work in progress.
.PP
The header suppresses the stack protector around its own code, because
GCC\(aqs canary prologue clobbers the register the trampoline delivers
the object pointer in.
The suppression is pushed and popped, so it does not leak into the
translation unit that included the header.
